# InterGenOS — explicit-enable list.
# Services we deliberately want active by default on installed systems.
# Add new lines here when you want a service auto-enabled on first boot;
# add nothing here for "user installs it, user enables it" semantics.
#
# Already covered by other preset files:
#   - gdm.service          (90-gdm.preset)
#   - nftables.service     (90-nftables.preset)
#   - systemd-* services   (90-systemd.preset, upstream)

enable NetworkManager.service
enable apparmor.service
enable systemd-oomd.service

# bluetooth.service — enabled by default to match desktop-distro convention.
# Survey (2026-06-10, GBC003 G3-9): Fedora, Debian, Ubuntu and openSUSE all ship
# bluez with `enable bluetooth.service` in their vendor preset (status reads
# "vendor preset: enabled"); Arch is the lone outlier, leaving it disabled under
# its minimal-defaults philosophy (the same deny-by-default stance our whitelist
# otherwise follows). InterGenOS ships GNOME as a desktop, and a fresh user
# expects BT mice/keyboards/headphones to just work — without this line the
# install-time `systemctl preset-all` reverts the unit via the 99- `disable *`
# catch-all (observed on a development machine: "Removed bluetooth.target.wants/bluetooth.service")
# leaving bluetoothd inert and GNOME's Bluetooth toggle non-functional.
# Security note: enabling the unit starts bluetoothd but does NOT make the
# adapter discoverable/pairable — GNOME keeps the adapter non-discoverable until
# the user opens Settings, so the surface is the local daemon, not an open
# pairing window (the standard desktop posture). bluez ships in the desktop tier
# (always present with the required GNOME stack); the line is a no-op if absent.
enable bluetooth.service

# (The G3-3 intergenos-greeter-background.service was retired in GBC003.1 —
# its Before=gdm ordering edge caused the AMD first-boot GDM crash (PI-14), and
# the greeter background is now set in the gnome-shell theme CSS, not via a
# boot-time selector. No whitelist line is needed.)

# pkm-check-updates.timer is the SOLE producer of
# /var/lib/pkm/available-updates.json, which the default-enabled
# pkm-notifier@intergenos.org GNOME extension (and the motd-pkm-pending hook)
# read to surface available (security) updates. pkm/build.sh enables the timer
# at image-build time, but the install-time `systemctl preset-all` (users.py)
# reverts it via the 99- `disable *` catch-all unless it is whitelisted here --
# so without this line the update-notifier ships enabled but permanently inert
# (its JSON is never written, and a timer cannot be D-Bus-activated as a
# fallback). Update awareness is security-relevant. (GBC002.5, 2026-06-08.)
enable pkm-check-updates.timer

# logrotate.timer rotates the system logs, including
# /etc/logrotate.d/intergen-tool-dispatch — the snippet the tree has shipped
# since InterGen's tool-dispatch audit log landed, with nothing consuming it
# until the logrotate package existed. Without this line the install-time
# `systemctl preset-all` reverts the timer through the 99- `disable *`
# catch-all, so the rotation would ship present and permanently inert: exactly
# the failure recorded for pkm-check-updates.timer above. An audit log that
# grows without bound is a disk-exhaustion path on a long-lived install, and a
# log nothing rotates is also a log nobody can keep.
enable logrotate.timer

# switcheroo-control.service is the D-Bus service GNOME Shell asks which GPU an
# application should run on; it is what puts "Launch using Discrete Graphics
# Card" in the application menu on hybrid-graphics machines. Its unit is
# Type=dbus with WantedBy=graphical.target, so without a whitelist line the
# 99- `disable *` catch-all leaves it installed and never started, and the menu
# entry is silently absent with no error anywhere to explain why.
enable switcheroo-control.service

# NetworkManager-wait-online.service — enabled, stated here rather than left
# implied. Decided 2026-08-19.
#
# It was already enabled on every install, but not by anything that said so:
# NetworkManager.service's own [Install] section carries
# `Also=NetworkManager-wait-online.service`, so whitelisting NetworkManager
# above enables the wait unit as a side effect. Measured by running the
# installer's own pass (`systemctl --root <target> preset-all`) against a root
# holding these preset files and the real shipped units: preset-all first
# removed the wait-online link, then re-created it while enabling
# NetworkManager. The effective answer therefore depended on the order units
# were visited inside a single preset-all run. Naming the unit here makes the
# result a stated decision instead of an ordering artefact, and it agrees with
# `Also=` rather than fighting it, so the two can never disagree.
#
# Survey: upstream systemd ships the same posture for its own network stack —
# presets/90-systemd.preset carries `enable systemd-networkd-wait-online.service`.
# The packaging of the same unit elsewhere has already moved the decision out of
# the package and into the preset for exactly this reason; one distribution's
# NetworkManager packaging still carries the comment "older versions used to
# install this file, effectively always enabling
# NetworkManager-wait-online.service. We no longer do that and rely on preset."
#
# Cost, measured on an installed system rather than argued: the unit is
# `WantedBy=network-online.target`, and on a default install nothing pulls that
# target in — its reverse-dependency list holds only itself, and the three units
# that declare `Wants=network-online.target` (systemd-sysupdate.service,
# transmission-daemon.service, update-pki.service) are all left disabled by the
# 99- catch-all, with update-pki.timer disabled too. The unit did not run at all
# on the measured boot: inactive, no start timestamp, and absent from
# `systemd-analyze blame`. When something does pull the target, the unit is
# bounded, not open-ended: it ships `Environment=NM_ONLINE_TIMEOUT=60` and is
# Type=oneshot, so the worst case is a 60-second wait after which boot
# continues. Two descriptions of this unit in the tree were wrong in opposite
# directions and are removed by the same change set: one called it a service
# that "blocks boot indefinitely", the other put its worst case at 30 seconds,
# which is the nm-online binary's default and not the value the shipped unit
# sets.
#
# Security note: the unit runs `nm-online -s -q`, which waits for
# NetworkManager to report startup complete. It opens no port, accepts no
# input, and starts no daemon of its own.
#
# Consequence of reverting this line: the unit goes back to being enabled only
# as a side effect of `Also=`, which is the state this entry exists to stop
# being unstated. To genuinely stop the unit from being enabled, the
# `Also=NetworkManager-wait-online.service` line in NetworkManager.service has
# to be addressed as well — a `disable` line here would contend with it inside
# the same preset-all run and reintroduce the ordering dependence described
# above.
enable NetworkManager-wait-online.service

# Explicit disables — override the upstream 90-systemd.preset, which `enable`s
# systemd-networkd. Preset resolution is first-match-wins across lexically-
# sorted files, so these 80- lines beat 90-systemd.preset's enable (and are
# reached before the 99- `disable *` catch-all). NetworkManager (above) owns
# networking on InterGenOS; systemd-networkd is redundant, and its
# wait-online unit delays network-online.target (~6s observed on the GBC001.4
# boot), which gates GDM. NM provides DHCP via its own backend.
# networkmanager/build.sh disables networkd at package-build time, but a later
# `systemctl preset-all` (build-squashfs customize hook) re-enabled it from
# 90-systemd.preset — these lines close that loop durably. (GBC001.5,
# 2026-06-05.)
disable systemd-networkd.service
disable systemd-networkd.socket
disable systemd-networkd-wait-online.service

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Explicit default-off decisions. Decided 2026-08-19.
#
# These three would already resolve to disabled through the 99- `disable *`
# catch-all, so the lines below change no resolved state. They are written out
# because a default that is only the absence of a whitelist line reads as an
# oversight, and each of these has in fact been read that way: the recipes for
# all three called `systemctl enable` on their own unit, which the install-time
# `systemctl preset-all` then reverted, so the tree stated one policy and shipped
# another. Those calls are removed in the same change set that adds these lines,
# leaving these files the single place a default is decided.
#
# Survey: a package deciding its own default is the pattern being retired here,
# not the convention. One distribution ships a global `disable *` of exactly the
# same shape as the 99- file. Another hands every one of these four packages'
# enablement to its distribution preset policy through the packaging macro that
# runs `systemctl preset` at install time, so the package names the units and the
# preset decides them.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# cups — the print scheduler ships off, because printing is one of the
# opt-in switches the welcome application offers on first run. That page tells
# the user in as many words that the services on it "ship OFF for security", and
# its printing switch runs `systemctl enable --now cups.socket`. A default-on
# scheduler would make that sentence untrue and the switch meaningless, since it
# would already be on before the user saw it.
#
# Activation posture, stated honestly: disabled here does not mean unreachable.
# cups.service is `TriggeredBy=cups.socket`, so once the user turns printing on,
# the socket starts the scheduler on the first request. With printing off,
# neither unit is enabled and nothing listens. Measured on an installed system
# where the user had turned printing on: cups.service reads disabled and is
# nevertheless running, reached through the enabled socket, and cupsd listens on
# 127.0.0.1:631 and [::1]:631 only — loopback, never a network interface.
#
# Security note: the exposure a running scheduler adds is local, not remote,
# because of that loopback binding. The reason to leave it off is not an open
# port; it is that a print daemon, its filters and its driver stack are a large
# amount of code to run on a machine that may never print.
#
# Consequence of reverting these lines: the scheduler starts on every install
# whether or not the machine has a printer, and the welcome application's
# printing switch shows as already on at first run while claiming it ships off.
disable cups.service
disable cups.socket
disable cups.path

# avahi — the mDNS/DNS-SD responder ships off for the same reason and one
# further one. It is the welcome application's "Enable Network Discovery"
# switch, which runs `systemctl enable --now avahi-daemon.service` AND writes a
# firewall fragment accepting udp/5353; turning it back off removes that
# fragment again. The firewall's input chain is `policy drop`, so the daemon and
# the rule that lets it answer are deliberately turned on together by one
# choice.
#
# Activation posture, stated honestly: avahi has no socket or D-Bus activation
# path that would start it while disabled. Disabled here means the daemon does
# not run.
#
# Security note: this is the one of the three that changes what the machine says
# about itself on a network. The daemon binds 0.0.0.0:5353 and [::]:5353 —
# measured on an installed system with discovery turned on — and mDNS is an
# announcement protocol: a default-on responder would advertise the machine's
# hostname and services to every network it ever joins, including untrusted
# ones, without the user having asked for it. Enabling it by default would also
# leave the daemon running while the firewall still dropped inbound udp/5353, so
# the machine would broadcast and be unable to answer: attack surface with the
# feature still not working.
#
# Consequence of reverting these lines: the machine announces itself on every
# network it joins from first boot, and the welcome application's discovery
# switch shows as already on while its firewall half is still absent.
disable avahi-daemon.service
disable avahi-daemon.socket

# rtkit — the realtime-scheduling broker ships ON. Decided 2026-08-19,
# revising the disable this file first carried: a desktop's audio stack asks
# for realtime scheduling in ordinary use, so the daemon runs on effectively
# every install either way — the package ships
# /usr/share/dbus-1/system-services/org.freedesktop.RealtimeKit1.service
# declaring `SystemdService=rtkit-daemon.service`, and that D-Bus activation
# starts the unit on first request regardless of the enablement flag
# (measured on an installed system: flag disabled, unit running).
#
# A written default that says one thing while the machine reliably does
# another is a state the user cannot read the truth from. Enabled makes the
# stated default and the actual behaviour the same thing, at the cost of
# starting at boot what first use would have started moments later.
#
# Consequence of reverting this line: the flag reads disabled while the
# daemon still runs on any system with an audio stack — the misleading state
# this entry exists to remove.
enable rtkit-daemon.service

# at and fcron — the two job schedulers ship off. Decided 2026-08-19.
#
# Neither was named when this change set started; both surfaced from the test
# that came with it, which resolves every remaining `systemctl enable` in the
# tree against the real preset engine and reports the ones the policy
# contradicts. Both recipes enabled their own daemon while the policy disabled
# it, which is the same shape as the four above.
#
# These two are also where the cost of leaving that unresolved was measurable.
# On a fresh install the preset pass runs last and the daemons ship off. But
# nothing re-runs that pass after an ordinary package operation, so on a machine
# where either package was later reinstalled or upgraded, the recipe's enable
# was the last word and the daemon has been running ever since. Measured on a
# development machine installed 2026-08-03: both units read STATE enabled with
# PRESET disabled, and their enablement symlinks carry a timestamp three days
# after the install, from a package operation and not from the install. Two
# machines from the same medium therefore held different answers, and no file
# in the tree recorded which.
#
# Off is what a fresh install already ships, so these lines change nothing about
# a new machine; what they change is that both paths now agree, and that the
# answer is written down.
#
# Survey: neither daemon is enabled by the global default of the distribution
# whose preset posture matches ours (a single `disable *`). Beyond that, this is
# not a convention question — running two job schedulers by default on a desktop
# is the part that needs justifying, and nothing in this tree asks for it. The
# one scheduled job the system does rely on, log rotation, runs from
# logrotate.timer, which is whitelisted above on its own merits.
#
# Activation posture, stated honestly: neither has socket or D-Bus activation.
# Disabled means the daemon does not run, and an `at` or `fcron` job a user
# creates will not fire until they enable the corresponding unit.
#
# Security note: a job scheduler runs arbitrary user-supplied commands as root
# or as the submitting user on a timer. Both packages also ship setuid and
# setgid binaries, which their own install hooks repair on every deployment.
# That surface is worth having available and is not worth running by default on
# a machine that has been given no jobs to run.
#
# Consequence of reverting these lines: both daemons start on every install.
# Turning either on by default is a one-line change here — which is the point of
# deciding it in one file instead of in a recipe that the install-time pass
# silently overrules.
disable atd.service
disable fcron.service

# forge-tui.service has ConditionKernelCommandLine=igos.mode=install-tui
# so it only fires in install-tui mode. In live + install-gui modes the
# condition is not met and the unit is a no-op. Enabling here so it's
# in multi-user.target.wants/ for install-tui boots to find.
enable forge-tui.service
